WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.250
Continue in the same evaluation ring
A, with u odd and greater than one.

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:08.500
At each subset root zeta sub S,

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:14.880
abbreviate the evaluated polynomials
by c sub S, d sub S, and g sub S.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:20.320
The two ratio families are r sub
S equal to d sub S over c sub S,

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:23.840
and w sub S equal to g sub S over c sub S.

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:28.540
The sign array already supplied
exact polynomial quotients:

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:34.160
d minus c divided by two, and g
minus c divided by one plus i.

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:37.240
First perform that
coefficientwise division.

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:41.040
Then evaluate at zeta sub S.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:45.080
Finally multiply by the
unit inverse of c sub S.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:48.200
Both resulting elements belong to A.

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:53.400
We have introduced no inverse
of two or of one plus i.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:59.700
Write each ratio as one plus
its difference from one.

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:02.830
For the r family, the multiplier is two;

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:06.420
for the w family, it is one plus i.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.940
Call the corresponding
divided difference x sub S.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:15.800
Both rows now have the exact
form one plus t times x sub S,

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:17.900
with x sub S in A.

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:22.660
The value of t and the elements
x sub S depend on the family.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:25.360
Start with two factors.

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:29.220
Choose one or t x one
from the first factor,

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:32.100
and one or t x two from the second.

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:36.887
The four choices give
one, t x one, t x two,

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:41.660
and t squared x one x two.
Collect the two linear terms.

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:47.967
This is an equality: one plus t
times the sum of x one and x two,

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:51.212
plus t squared x one x two.

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:56.560
With three factors, choose either
one or t x from each factor again.

00:01:57.280 --> 00:01:59.100
Choosing no x gives one.

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:04.340
Choosing exactly one gives t times
the sum of the three x values.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:07.700
Choosing two gives three quadratic terms.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:13.260
Choosing all three gives the cubic term.
Keep those last four terms together.

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:19.600
They equal t squared times Q, where Q is
the sum of the three pairwise products,

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:24.440
plus t times the triple product.
Nothing has been discarded.

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:29.930
The same grouping works for any finite
collection. The product equals one,

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:35.720
plus t times the sum of all x values,
plus t squared Q, with Q in A.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:39.120
Every remaining term contains
at least two copies of t.

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:42.200
This is an exact remainder,
not an approximation.

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:45.320
For one factor, Q is zero.

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:50.540
Apply this identity to each side
of the even-subset and odd-subset

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:55.240
product equality separately. Keep their
linear sums and their remainders distinct.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:59.640
Use t equal to two for the
r ratios and t equal to one

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:03.660
plus i for the w ratios, with the
corresponding x values in each case.

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:08.380
For u equal to fifteen,
each side has two factors.

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:13.160
For u equal to three, each side has
just one, so both remainders vanish.

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:17.640
The next step subtracts the two
expanded products and cancels a

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:21.700
common factor in the original
ring before any modular reduction.
